    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d141">

                <pb facs="0233" n="131" sameAs="frus:frus1927v03/pg131"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.918/208</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Castle</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Italian
                        Ambassador</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Martino</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1928-01-12"><hi rendition="#italic">January 12,
                        1928</hi></date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">My Dear Mr. Ambassador</hi>: I have received your
                    letter of January 4, 1928, in which you call my attention to the translation of
                    an article stated to have been published in the December 8, 1927 issue of <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Il Nuovo Mondo</hi>, attacking the Italian
                    Government.</p>
                <p>I deeply regret that any newspaper published in this country should permit the
                    political opinions of its staff regarding the conduct of a foreign Government to
                    be publicly expressed in the foolish and intemperate language reproduced in your
                    note under acknowledgment.</p>
                <p>I have, however, no option but to point out that, as in the previous case
                    mentioned by you, there would be no likelihood, under our existing laws, of
                    successful prosecution in the instance now quoted by you.</p>
                <p>Very sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">W. R. Castle</hi>, Jr.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
